[Music]  while working as the archivist of my  collection of curiosities i've  researched a lot of cryptids famous  monsters like bigfoot and the yeti but  also lesser-known creatures like the  sheep squatch and the loveland frogman  as a methodical archivist i have to  admit that a lot of the evidence for  such creatures as amazing as they are  can be somewhat questionable  indeed there's a long and frankly  hilarious history of people trying and  failing to fake the existence of these  cryptids to the masses so today i'll be  unveiling some of the greatest  historical cryptid hoaxes and while the  monsters on this list are fake the real  stories behind their fabrication are  often just as outlandish as the  creatures themselves involving lies  double crossing and ridiculous amounts  of money down the drain  so let's shine a light on these stories  in the summer of 1937 reports of giant  footprints on the shores of nantucket  shocked the tiny u.s town  photographs of what appeared to be the  tracks of a large unknown animal soon  followed stories spread that a sea  serpent right out of ancient myth had  come ashore somewhere on the island a  few days later an account came in that a  gigantic monster was spotted on the  beach and huge crowds gathered to  investigate and they were greeted by  this  a sea serpent to be sure but clearly not  a real one this hilariously fake  creature was the creation of this guy  tony sarg an early pioneer in puppetry  who had orchestrated the whole thing  footprints included to get the town and  himself into the news  although even at the time people  obviously weren't fooled by the giant  inflatable monster once they actually  saw it sarg's self-promotion plan was a  success later that year he'd actually  end up designing the first balloons of  the macy's day parade which his fake sea  serpent also flew in  the nantucket sea serpent is a pretty  amusing and harmless hoax all in all  but not all hoaxes have such pleasant  backstories in new york in the early  1840s a reported 10 000 pamphlets swept  the city claiming the body of a  real-life mermaid had been found and was  going on display  the location of this supposed unveiling  was a museum at the corner of broadway  and park row which just so happened to  be owned by one p.t barnum the infamous  showman conman and all-around  questionable dude and this mermaid was  right out of his scheming playbook  though advertised as a beautiful being  from classical myth the actual specimen  people would see was a strange shriveled  creature that horrified onlookers this  is because barnum's mermaid was of  course not a supernatural creature but a  hoax the object was in fact composed of  the body and torso of a taxidermied  monkey attached to the back half of a  fish in a pretty twisted attempt to  create a natural looking life form while  the scientific community at large  debunked the specimen multiple times the  mermaid was still a huge success for  barnum with people from all over coming  to examine it  the hoax met its end when it was likely  destroyed in one of the many fires that  destroyed other parts of barnum's  collections and so the mermaid scam was  no more  but other cryptid hoaxes were far more  enduring perhaps no single faked image  has influenced more people than this  famous 1934 photograph that supposedly  captures the one and only loch ness  monster this photo is a huge part of  what launched the supposed sea monster  into popular culture and led to  countless follow-up articles and other  alleged sightings it wasn't until much  later that the picture was officially  exposed as a fake and the tale behind  why it was taken is truly ridiculous  the story of the hoax begins in 1933  when the newspaper the daily mail hired  one marmaduke wetherell pictured here  looking extremely normal to track down  the beast a british actor turned  eccentric big game hunter weatherell  reported a series of footprints  belonging to the creature yes footprints  at this time the concept of the loch  ness monster being fully aquatic wasn't  completely in the public consciousness  and a lot of supposed sightings from the  era involved the beast lumbering across  dry land wetherill's footprints were  ridiculed by the daily mail when it  turned out he had faked them by pressing  the taxidermied leg of a hippo into the  sand looking to get back at the  newspaper wetherill paid his son-in-law  to take a photo of a wooden dinosaur  head glued to a toy submarine from a  distance this hoax made for a decently  convincing monster and the rest is  history  not all cryptid hoaxes were so cheap to  produce however others like the  petrified cardiff giant were extremely  costly and complex undertakings and  deception the grand illusion began two  years before the mysterious giant was  unveiled when an american named george  hull hired a german sculptor in secret  to carve a ten foot or three meter long  block of stone into the likeness of a  giant he then transported the stone  giant by a railroad to the form of his  cousin where he buried it nearly a year  later hull hired two men to build a well  where he knew his fake petrified giant  had been buried in total this elaborate  plan likely cost over 51 000  in today's money but hull soon made his  money back as people from across the  country scrambled to take a look at the  supposed giant the giant drew such  crowds that another con man hired  someone to model the giant shape and wax  and create a plaster replica that conman  was pt barnum again showing that guy was  really everywhere  once barnum started making money off his  copy of the giant hold tried to sue  barnum in court calling barnum's giant a  fake reportedly the judge told hull to  get his giant to swear on its own  genuineness if he wanted a favorable  ruling in the end both giants were  revealed as fakes in court quite the  series of twists  other equally wild hoaxes emerged from  much more peaceful backgrounds the  cottingly fairies appear in infamous  photographs taken in 1917 near the small  english village of cottingley these  photographs feature what appear to be  various fairies interacting with two  young cousins and are extremely bold  compared to many of the other hoaxes by  not being blurry in the slightest these  photographs had a profound effect on the  public which at the time was  experiencing a rise in interest in  psychic phenomena as part of an escape  from the horrors of the first world war  sir arthur conan doyle most famous as  the author of the sherlock holmes  stories was a particularly passionate  advocate that the photos were genuine  many were more skeptical however as  close examination of the photos made it  seem pretty clear the fairies were  actually just paper cutouts positioned  so they appeared to be interacting with  the children while believing the photos  were real might have been the far more  appealing option interests soon died  down and eventually the two cousins  admitted that the photographs were  cardboard cutouts copied from popular  children's books of the time nonetheless  it's a fascinating moment of history  not all hoaxes were debunked so easily  however the pilt-down man is perhaps the  greatest hoax on this list in terms of  how long it was accepted by the  scientific community though not  encrypted in the traditional sense this  fossil supposedly belonged to a missing  link between apes and humans the tale of  this missing link begins in 1912 when  charles dawson an amateur scientist who  should not be confused with the way  cooler charles darwin claimed to have  found a skull that would fill a supposed  gap in paleontology regarding human  origins the majority of scholars in this  emerging field believed his finding to  be legitimate and reconstructions of the  piltdown man became famous  nationalism and cultural prejudice  played a role in this as the piltdown  man satisfied the expectations of  british scientists that the earliest  humans must have emerged in europe even  at the time however more thorough  paleontologists were skeptical from the  outset with some suggesting the skull  was actually just the cranium of a human  stuck onto the jawbone of an orangutan  this assertion turned out to be exactly  correct and was proved definitively in  1953 with charles dawson being the  likely culprit behind the forgery from  the outset the missing link concept of  the piltdown man was grounded in an  outdated understanding of evolution as  something strictly linear and  progressive our modern phylogenetic tree  of hominid evolution is way way more  complex with there being no solitary  missing link between anatomically modern  humans and our anthropoid ancestors the  pill down man is therefore an even more  obvious hoax in retrospect  and the last hoax on our list is  something more harmless and quite a bit  more recent the pacific northwest tree  octopus is encrypted from the early  internet age it emerged on the scene  with this website which encouraged  readers to help save the endangered  tree-dwelling octopus species of the  temperate rainforests in north america  the tree octopus is obviously just a fun  joke but thanks to the many doctored  photographs on display a lot of people  thought this was a real animal this  website actually led to early  discussions on how easily the internet  can trick people into believing false  information an idea that is commonplace  now  the creator of this hoax who goes by the  pseudonym lyle zappado is an early  mastermind of surrealist internet humor  and i've dropped a link to his site and  social media in the description below  because out of all these cryptids the  tree octopus is definitely the most fun  and my personal favorite  and with that i've covered some of the  craziest hoax stories i've found while  doing research for the archive if there  are other cryptid hoaxes that you're a  fan of please let me know in the  comments  also thanks for watching if you enjoyed  this entry please lend your support and  like subscribe and hit the notification  icon to stay up to date on all things  curious see you in the next video  you

